                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Richard & Alice is a story-driven point-and-click adventure game that could be considered a thriller and a mystery title. However, it leaves too much to the observer's interpretation, which made me think it’s either rushed or falls short without the developers ' intentions. \r\n\r\nAs the title implies, you play as two protagonists called Richard and Alice who ended up in prison for different reasons after the world fell victim to the endless snowfall that caused a dystopian state and forced people to live in dedicated safe zones, rendering the majority of the Earth uninhabitable. \r\n\r\nThe story mostly revolves around Alice and her son since you witness her backstory in detail, whereas Richard’s is mostly left to interpretation, but both of them have been pushed into a corner by the harsh reality and have done questionable things. \r\n\r\nIt’s a very tragic tale that explores the idea of faith and its necessity in human life, and how morals take shape depending on the comfort of the individuals. The fragility of human nature is conveyed so well that it becomes a memorable experience for this reason alone.\r\n\r\nIt doesn’t take long to see all the endings, and it doesn’t cost that much; therefore, it’s definitely worth your time. It’d be wise not to expect much from gameplay for obvious reasons. \r\n\r\n[h1]TL;DR[/h1]\r\nA tragic story about two people who were pushed into a corner by the harsh reality of a post-apocalyptic world, but the ending is left to interpretation a bit too much.",
